it's decision day in Nigeria from a list  of 30 candidates the choice for seven  and a half million voters is now  narrowed to only two familiar faces of  the country's political landscape  Muhammad bazum a former minister is the  ruling Party candidate  he pulled the most vote in the first  round of presidential election last year  with 39.3 percent of the vote  his opponent mamanusman came a distant  second with less than half the votes of  the front runner  the former prime minister and president  is looking to come back to a job he left  25 years ago  Osman is the only opposition candidate  who's not collaborated with the ruling  pnts the others did several times all  big names in Nigeria politics have at  one time or another gone into some form  of alliance with the ruling party  but whoever wins will need to form a  coalition to govern that looks a lot  easier for the ruling pnds party that  won the most seats in Parliament in  December but sixth short of a working  majority  The Early Air has only seven seats in  the 171 seat Parliament  so ahead of Sunday's vote there have  been negotiations between the two  candidates and smaller parties to form a  coalition  the election commission says it expects  the election to be conducted smoothly  but there are fears of attacks by armed  groups in some areas in December the  country missed the opportunity to  produce a president in the first round  of voting but is still on calls to Mark  a historic Milestone Sunday's vote is  expected to lead to the first peaceful  transition of power from one civilian  government to another in Niger  but whoever wins will face daunting  challenges poverty made worse by  covid-19 is on the rise youth  unemployment is high so also is a level  of illiteracy but more worrying is the  violence on the borders  in the west bokaram has raided many  communities and along the border with  Mali and Burkina Faso groups allied with  isil and Al-Qaeda are attacking civilian  and Military targets Ahmad Idris  al-juzira
 

